'''VideoMes''' is an [[Abyala]]n online {{w|Video hosting service|video sharing platform}} headquartered in [[Sant Cristòfor]], [[Balboa]]. Founded in 2009, it is the dominant video sharing service in southern Abyala, with approximately 80 million registered users as of 2025, concentrated primarily in [[Tapuya]], [[Balboa]], [[Lacashe]], [[Potocsí]], and [[Asikyira]].{{sfn|DigitalAbyala|2025|p=44}}

The platform is particularly popular in [[Volisanian language|Volisanian]]- and [[Burawi language|Burawi]]-speaking communities on the continent, and its content ecosystem — spanning music, comedy, gossip, news, sports, and lifestyle — has made it a central cultural institution in the regions it serves.{{sfn|DigitalAbyala|2025|p=46}}

== History ==

=== Founding and early years (2009–2012) ===

VideoMes was founded on 15 March 2009 by [[Ricard Serra]], a Volisanian-Balboan software developer and former university lecturer in computer science at the [[Universitat de l'Istme]] in Sant Cristòfor.{{sfn|Serra|2018|p=12}} Serra, who had observed the rapid growth of global video sharing platforms but noted the absence of a service tailored to the linguistic and cultural needs of southern Abyala, developed the platform as a regional alternative that would support Volisanian, Burawi, and other Abyalan languages at a time when major international platforms offered limited localisation.{{sfn|Serra|2018|p=14}}

The platform launched with a focus on user-generated content — comedy sketches, music videos, vlogs, and sports highlights — and gained early traction in Sant Cristòfor's creative community, where young filmmakers and musicians adopted it as a free distribution channel.{{sfn|DigitalAbyala|2025|p=48}} By the end of 2009, VideoMes had approximately 200,000 registered users, almost exclusively in Balboa.{{sfn|Serra|2018|p=20}}

=== Growth and regional expansion (2012–2018) ===

The platform's growth accelerated significantly between 2012 and 2018, driven by three factors: the expansion of mobile internet access across southern Abyala, the emergence of a generation of content creators who built their careers on the platform, and the addition of Burawi-language support in 2013, which opened the platform to users in [[Lacashe]] and [[Asikyira]], and Volisanian-language localisation for [[Potocsí]].{{sfn|DigitalAbyala|2025|p=50}}

During this period, VideoMes became the primary platform for several cultural phenomena that defined southern Abyalan popular culture: the [[Balboan comedy]] scene, which produced a generation of sketch comedians who achieved mainstream fame through the platform; the [[Volisanian indie music]] movement, which used VideoMes as its primary distribution channel; and the emergence of celebrity gossip and entertainment shows, such as [[Ella Isthmus]], which began on VideoMes in 2012 before transitioning to television.{{sfn|DigitalAbyala|2025|p=52}}

By 2016, VideoMes had 35 million users and had expanded its operations to offices in [[Volis (city)|Volis]], [[Tapuya]], and [[Port-Guilhem]], [[Lacashe]].{{sfn|Serra|2018|p=45}} The platform introduced a monetisation programme for creators in 2015, which allowed popular channels to earn revenue from advertising, and a premium subscription service in 2017.{{sfn|DigitalAbyala|2025|p=54}}

=== Maturity and competition (2018–present) ===

By 2018, VideoMes had established itself as the dominant video platform in its core markets, but faced increasing competition from global platforms seeking to expand into Abyala.{{sfn|DigitalAbyala|2025|p=56}} The platform responded by investing in original content production, launching [[VideoMes Originals]] in 2019 — a slate of funded series and documentaries produced exclusively for the platform — and by expanding its live-streaming capabilities, which proved particularly popular for sports coverage and music events.{{sfn|DigitalAbyala|2025|p=58}}

In 2022, VideoMes reached 70 million users and introduced support for [[Waajuri language|Waajuri]], further expanding its reach into [[Asikyira]].{{sfn|DigitalAbyala|2025|p=60}} As of 2025, the platform has approximately 80 million users.{{sfn|DigitalAbyala|2025|p=44}}

== Features and content ==

VideoMes operates as a free-to-use video hosting platform with a freemium revenue model. Users can upload, view, and share videos without charge; a premium subscription, '''VideoMes+''', offers ad-free viewing, offline downloads, and access to exclusive content.{{sfn|DigitalAbyala|2025|p=62}}

The platform's content is organised into categories reflecting its core user base: music, comedy, sports, gossip and entertainment, news and current affairs, lifestyle, and education.{{sfn|DigitalAbyala|2025|p=64}} Music is the most-watched category, accounting for approximately 30% of total views, followed by comedy (22%) and sports (18%).{{sfn|DigitalAbyala|2025|p=65}}

=== Notable content and creators ===

VideoMes has been the launchpad for numerous creators and shows that have become cultural touchstones in southern Abyala:

* '''[[Ella Isthmus]]''' — a celebrity gossip programme founded by [[Aminata Diallo]] in 2012, which transitioned from VideoMes to television and became one of the most-watched entertainment shows in Balboa.{{sfn|DigitalAbyala|2025|p=68}}
* '''Comedy del Sud''' — a collective of Balboan and Lacashian sketch comedians whose videos regularly receive millions of views and who have been credited with popularising southern Abyalan humour across the continent.{{sfn|DigitalAbyala|2025|p=70}}
* '''Volis Sessions''' — a music series featuring live performances by Volisanian-language indie artists, recorded in the studios of Radio Volis in Sant Cristòfor, which has been credited with launching the careers of several prominent musicians.{{sfn|DigitalAbyala|2025|p=72}}

== Cultural impact ==

VideoMes has been described as the most significant cultural platform in southern Abyala since the expansion of regional television in the 1990s.{{sfn|DigitalAbyala|2025|p=76}} Its role in providing a platform for Volisanian- and Burawi-language content — languages that are underrepresented on global video platforms — has been credited with strengthening linguistic identity and cultural production in the regions it serves.{{sfn|Serra|2018|p=88}}

The platform has also been the subject of criticism. Some commentators have argued that its algorithmic recommendation system amplifies sensationalist content and gossip, and that its relatively lax moderation policies have allowed misinformation to spread on the platform.{{sfn|DigitalAbyala|2025|p=80}} Serra has defended the platform's moderation approach, stating that VideoMes employs content moderators fluent in all supported languages and that its policies are adapted to the cultural contexts of its user base.{{sfn|Serra|2018|p=92}}

